  ],
  "text": "Sugar runs on the same reward pathway as everything else your brain chases. Which is why one is rarely one. The mechanism is not willpower. Sweetness meant safe calories for most of our history, so your brain pays you for finding it. It releases dopamine. Not pleasure exactly. The signal that says get more of that. The problem is speed. Blood sugar spikes, and the correction overshoots below where you started. So an hour later you are hungrier than you were before you ate. Liquid sugar is the worst version. It arrives fastest and barely registers as food. You would never eat ten spoonfuls of sugar. You will drink them without noticing. And low fat products often replace the fat with sugar, because the taste had to come from somewhere. None of this is a character flaw. It is a system that worked, running in a world it was not built for. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
